-- js-attrs.rofl — WHAT THE SCANNER SAYS THAT NO RULE HEARS, and why.
--
-- `unconsumed_attr[audit]` in rules/js-attrs.rofl reported THIRTEEN keys on the
-- honest tree the day it was written, and `unconsumed_value[audit]` fourteen
-- (key, value) pairs. Every one of them is either excused with a MEASURED
-- reason or deferred to an item by name, on the `not_a_construct` /
-- `frame_deferred` precedent — a gate red on an honest checkout gets switched
-- off, and its absence is then invisible.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. A SECOND SPELLING OF A FACT THE MODEL ALREADY HAS. The criterion is
-- measurable rather than a preference: the distinction each attribute carries
-- is also carried by something the model reads, so a rule reading it would
-- derive nothing new.
attr_unread_ok(optional,   a_the_kind_carries_it).
-- `a?.b` — `optional_member_expression` and `optional_call_expression` are
-- separate KINDS, both declared, both gated on `optional_chaining`, and
-- `short_circuit_kind` reads them by name in rules/js-controlflow.rofl.
attr_unread_ok(method,     a_the_kind_carries_it).
-- `{ m() {} }` is `object_method` and `{ m: function () {} }` is an
-- `object_property` holding a `function_expression`. `fn_kind` reads the first
-- by name.
attr_unread_ok(tail,       a_the_child_index_carries_it).
-- whether a template quasi is the last one — `ast_child(T, quasis, I, Q)`
-- already gives the position, and the template rules read it.
attr_unread_ok(value_raw,  a_the_cooked_value_is_the_one_every_layer_reads).
-- the literal AS WRITTEN beside its cooked value. `may_be_lit` reads `value`,
-- and `value_cooked` is the template's own arm; the raw text differs from both
-- only in escapes, which no layer models.
attr_unread_ok(shorthand,  a_a_spelling_and_not_a_structure).
-- `{ a }` against `{ a: a }`. The key and the value are the same node in the
-- first and two nodes in the second, and every rule here reads them through
-- `ast_child`, which answers identically.
attr_unread_ok(declare,    a_the_era_table_gates_type_syntax_and_no_layer_models_it).
-- TypeScript's ambient modifier. `type_syntax` is a FEATURE with no year,
-- reachable only through `provides(ts5, type_syntax)`; no layer has an opinion
-- about a declaration that emits no code.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. NOBODY HAS READ IT YET, and the item is named. These are not spellings —
-- each carries something the model cannot otherwise derive.
attr_deferred(generator,   w_unconsumed_attribute).
attr_deferred(await,       w_unconsumed_attribute).
-- whether a function is a generator, whether a `for-of` is `for await`. The
-- control-flow layer models suspension through
-- `transfer_mechanism(await_expression, suspend)` and reads the KIND; these two
-- say the same thing about the FUNCTION and about the loop, which no kind
-- carries.
--
-- `async` WAS THE THIRD OF THIS GROUP AND THE ROW WAS FALSE WHEN IT WAS
-- WRITTEN. Measured 2026-09-09: `outside_attr_needs(js, await_expression, async,
-- true, top_level_await)` in facts/js-env.rofl is read by `within_attr[audit]`,
-- 398 rows over this corpus, and it is what tells ES2022 top-level await apart
-- from the same word inside an ES2017 async function. The deferral said *nobody
-- has read it yet* about a fact the era layer has been reading since 2026-09-05.
--
-- IT IS THE FIFTH INSTANCE OF THE SHAPE THIS ITEM IS NAMED FOR, and the first
-- one found by an instrument instead of by a reader: the note was written while
-- looking at the rule packs this audit's world happened to load, and
-- rules/js-env.rofl was not one of them. `attr_table_read` below is the
-- derivation that sees it and `attr_deferred_read[audit]` is the gate that
-- refuses the row.
attr_deferred(prefix,      w_unconsumed_attribute).
-- `i++` against `++i`. w_update_and_literals recorded that an update's VALUE
-- differs between the two forms and that the value layer carries neither; this
-- attribute is the half the scanner already supplies.
attr_deferred(pattern,     w_unconsumed_attribute).
attr_deferred(flags,       w_unconsumed_attribute).
-- THE REGEXP'S OWN TEXT, and the pair is worth a sentence because a mutant was
-- filed against it. w_update_and_literals left `literal_kind(reg_exp_literal)`
-- as a deliberate survivor, categorised `unkillable by the fact vocabulary: a
-- RegExpLiteral carries pattern/flags and no value, so the premise cannot be
-- populated`, and added `becomes killable the day the scanner records a
-- regexp's text`. THAT DAY WAS ALREADY PAST: the scanner records it, under
-- these two keys, and what is missing is a rule that reads them rather than a
-- fact. The category was right and the note's condition was already met.
attr_deferred(source_type, w_unconsumed_attribute).
-- `"module"` for every file in the corpus, because scanners/js_ast.ts hardcodes
-- `sourceType: 'module'`. Measured 2026-09-08 across all 34 fixtures under
-- `unambiguous`: three would be chosen as SCRIPT and the ONLY differing key in
-- the whole AST is this one. So the attribute is a constant today and the
-- moment the setting changes it stops being one — and every rule that silently
-- assumes strict mode becomes conditional on a row nobody reads.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. A VALUE NO RULE NAMES, where the key is only ever read with constants.
-- `name`, `value`, `kind` and `value_cooked` are absent from this list and
-- cannot appear in it: some rule reads each of them with the value LEFT FREE,
-- which is a rule reading every value there is. That exclusion is derived
-- (`attr_key_read_free`) rather than listed, and without it this table would
-- carry four hundred and fifty-five names.
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "+",   a_no_layer_models_arithmetic).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "-",   a_no_layer_models_arithmetic).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "*",   a_no_layer_models_arithmetic).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "++",  a_no_layer_models_arithmetic).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "--",  a_no_layer_models_arithmetic).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "+=",  a_no_layer_models_arithmetic).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "<",   a_no_layer_models_comparison).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, ">",   a_no_layer_models_comparison).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, ">=",  a_no_layer_models_comparison).
attr_value_unread_ok(operator, "||",  a_the_kind_carries_the_short_circuit).
-- `logical_expression` is a `short_circuit_kind` and the layer reads the KIND;
-- WHICH operator it is changes nothing about the guard, and the era table reads
-- `operator` only for the ones that carry an era — `**`, `??`, `??=`, `||=`,
-- `&&=`. That is the whole of why this list is operators.
-- `===` AND `void` LEFT THIS LIST ON 2026-09-09 AND THE EXCUSE THEY LEFT IS THE
-- POINT. Both were excused here as values `no layer models`, and the effect
-- layer now models exactly them: `eff_op_inspects[code]` in rules/js-effects.rofl
-- names the five operator spellings that run NO USER CODE - `===`, `!==`,
-- `typeof`, `void`, `!` - and everything else converts an operand by
-- complement, which is what makes `a + b` a hidden call and `a === b` not one.
-- Removing the two rows rather than leaving them is the difference between an
-- excuse and a decoration: `unconsumed_value[audit]` needs neither
-- `attr_pair_read` nor `attr_value_unread_ok`, so a row kept beside a rule that
-- reads the value is a sentence that has stopped being true and that nothing
-- would ever object to.
--
-- AND THE OTHER TEN STAY, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT SAYS THE COMPLEMENT IS REAL.
-- `+`, `-`, `*`, `<`, `>` and `>=` ARE modelled by the effect layer and are
-- still excused here, because that layer names only the operators that run no
-- user code and takes the rest BY COMPLEMENT - so no rule anywhere writes those
-- six spellings, and `no layer models arithmetic` is still exactly true of the
-- VALUE even though a layer now models the construct. `++`, `--` and `+=` sit on
-- `update_expression` and `assignment_expression`, which no layer reads an
-- operator off, and `||` sits on `logical_expression`, whose KIND carries the
-- short circuit.
attr_value_unread_ok(export_kind, "value", a_the_erasure_marker_is_the_other_value).
attr_value_unread_ok(import_kind, "value", a_the_erasure_marker_is_the_other_value).
-- `"type"` is what `export_local` and the import rules read: it marks a binding
-- TypeScript erases. `"value"` is the default and says only `not erased`, which
-- is the absence of the marker rather than a fact of its own.
attr_value_unread_ok(async, false, a_the_absence_of_the_marker_is_not_a_fact).
-- THE SAME SHAPE ARRIVING FROM A TABLE. `attr_needs`/`outside_attr_needs` name
-- `async, true` and the era layer reads it; `async, false` is on every ordinary
-- function in the corpus and says only `not async`. The bridge below makes the
-- TRUE half a read pair and this row is the FALSE half, on the same reasoning as
-- the two erasure markers above.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 4. THE TABLES THAT SUPPLY A KEY AT EVALUATION TIME. `attr_table_read` in
-- rules/js-attrs.rofl derives which relations these are — measured 2026-09-09,
-- exactly two over the seven JS rule packs, both at (Key=3, Value=4) — and
-- `attr_table_unbridged[audit]` refuses one that has no bridge rule. The rows
-- exist because Datalog cannot go from a relation's NAME to its ROWS, so the two
-- bridge rules are written by hand and this list is what keeps a third table
-- from arriving silently.
attr_table_bridged(attr_needs).
attr_table_bridged(outside_attr_needs).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 5. WHERE A GUARD'S POSITION HOLDS A KIND WITH NO SUCH ATTRIBUTE. `attr_slot_gap`
-- reads the guards out of the clauses: a constant-valued `ast_attr($var(S), K, _)`
-- premise whose subject is also the parent of an `ast_child($var(S), Field, ...)`
-- premise in the same rule. FOUR such guards exist and five rows fall out, of
-- which THREE are the audit's own coarseness and TWO are the real thing.
-- THE TWO ROWS THE ITEM EXISTS FOR ARE NOT ON THIS LIST AND MUST NOT BE.
-- `computed`/`key`/`class_private_property` and `.../class_private_method` are
-- real gaps in the corpus and `attr_slot_gap` reports them; they do not reach
-- `attr_blind_guard[audit]` because the only guard over that slot is a NEGATION
-- — `key_name` in rules/js-structure.rofl writes `not ast_attr(P, computed,
-- true)` deliberately, and its comment records the measurement. Excusing them
-- here would make the audit green under the POSITIVE form too, which is the one
-- mutant it exists to kill.
attr_slot_gap_ok(computed, property, meta_property,
                 a_the_rules_population_is_a_derived_relation).
-- `selects[flow]` binds N through `member_node_v[flow]`, which is
-- `member_expression` and `optional_member_expression` and nothing else. A
-- `meta_property` has a `property` child and no attributes at all — measured
-- again in rules/js-structure.rofl — but it is not in the population, and this
-- audit cannot narrow a population that arrives through a derived relation.
attr_slot_gap_ok(static, value, directive,        a_the_rules_population_is_a_mechanism_table).
attr_slot_gap_ok(static, value, object_property,  a_the_rules_population_is_a_mechanism_table).
-- `field_init[code]` binds P through `transfer_mechanism(K, per_construction)`
-- and `ast_node(P, K, _, _)` with K a VARIABLE, so the kind arm cannot pin it
-- the way it pins `delegates[flow]` to `yield_expression`. The two kinds here
-- have a `value` child and no `static`, and neither is a per-construction
-- member.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 6. AN ATTRIBUTE PRESENT ON SOME NODES OF A KIND AND ABSENT FROM OTHERS.
attr_split_ok(computed, class_private_method, a_babel_emits_it_for_the_accessor_forms_only).
-- ONE ROW, AND IT CORRECTS THE FINDING THAT ASKED FOR THIS AUDIT.
-- `f_a_positive_attribute_test_is_blind_to_a_kind_that_has_no_such_attribute`
-- explains the seven blind key positions as *babel gives a private name no such
-- field* — an assertion about a KIND. Measured 2026-09-09 over the three
-- `class_private_method` nodes in shapes.ts: `get #face` (line 667) carries
-- `computed, false` and `#rim` (666) and `#tucked` (836) carry no `computed` at
-- all. So the presence of the attribute depends on the value of ANOTHER
-- attribute on the same node — `kind` is `get` on the one that has it — and no
-- reading of the grammar BY KIND can say that. The excuse is that the guard this
-- affects is already the negation; the row is here so that the fact is written
-- down where the next reader of the slot audit will meet it.
